  "worked_solution": "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-{x}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z)=\\lambda \u2207g(x,y,z)+\\mu \u2207h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z)=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda \u2207g(x,y,z)+\\mu \u2207h(x,y,z) \\\\ \u27e8{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e8{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}\u27e9+\\mu \u27e8{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e82xy,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z\u27e9 & =\\lambda \u27e8-2x,\\,1,\\,0\u27e9+\\mu \u27e82x,\\,4y,\\,2z\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
